             \"description\": \"Output budget: 'tiny', 'compact', or 'full' (default auto chooses for you)\",\n325:                                \"description\": \"Optional approximate hard cap for the returned payload\"\n329:                                \"description\": \"Response wire format: 'standard' or 'dense' (optional; auto may choose dense for strict budgets)\",\n...\n</persisted-output>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbdQyD8DbWiNRxRFHe822D","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"9305cf57-6f72-4fb3-a0e1-077dda0983a2","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.370Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore extension + MCP surface"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:117:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QJgDabFv39X5VG3mqWABoS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Patch-Oriented Planning\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tPhase 3 adds `plan_edit`, a patch-oriented planning workflow that sits on top of the existing change-planning stack and returns a more edit-ready bundle for assistants. The goal is to move from \"here are the likely files\" to \"here is the first patch plan,\" while staying compact enough to use directly in an edit loop.\n6\t\n7\t## Why This Exists\n8\t\n9\t`prepare_change` is useful for finding the likely area to edit, but assistants still need additional structure before they can safely patch code:\n10\t\n11\t- which symbols are the best first edit anchors\n12\t- which line spans should be touched first\n13\t- which callers or dependency contracts are likely to move\n14\t- which docs and tests are likely to go stale\n15\t- which compact response is still small enough to apply without another discovery round\n16\t\n17\t`plan_edit` packages that information in one bundle so the assistant can move from task statement to concrete patch plan with fewer follow-up calls.\n18\t\n19\t## Bundle Shape\n20\t\n21\t`PlanEditBundle` currently includes:\n22\t\n23\t- `edit_files`\n24\t- `supporting_files`\n25\t- `symbols`\n26\t- `candidate_spans`\n27\t- `affected_callers`\n28\t- `affected_dependencies`\n29\t- `relevant_docs`\n30\t- `stale_doc_signals`\n31\t- `tests`\n32\t- `test_gaps`\n33\t- `matched_rules`\n34\t- `memories`\n35\t- `memory_highlights`\n36\t- `risks`\n37\t- `rationale`\n38\t- `stats` in full mode\n39\t\n40\tCandidate spans carry file, symbol, line range, confidence, and stable symbol handles when available. The daemon prefers the top candidate span handle for `suggested_expand`; when one is not available, it falls back to the underlying change-plan suggestion.\n41\t\n42\t## Compactness And Fallbacks\n43\t\n44\tThe daemon exposes both compact and full `plan_edit` delivery modes. Compact mode trims supporting material while preserving patch anchors, docs signals, and tests. Full mode keeps the wider stats bundle for inspection.\n45\t\n46\tThe same backward-compatible handle discipline used elsewhere in Lattice applies here as well:\n47\t\n48\t- stable symbol handles are preferred when the graph node is known\n49\t- legacy `symbol:` and `file:` forms remain accepted in the surrounding workflow plumbing\n50\t- cached context handles continue to work across `expand_context`\n51\t\n52\t## Current Implementation Touchpoints\n53\t\n54\tThe implementation now lives in:\n55\t\n56\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs`\n57\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/mod.rs`\n58\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`\n59\t\n60\tThe daemon layer adds the MCP tool declaration, request handling, compact/full shaping, context-handle seeding, and tests for `tools/call` behavior.\n61\t\n62\t## Verification Status\n63\t\n64\tManual consistency pass only for this note.\n65\t\n66\tVerified against the roadmap and the landed code that:\n67\t\n68\t- `plan_edit` exists as an MCP tool and daemon workflow\n69\t- the bundle includes edit files, candidate spans, caller/dependency impacts, docs guidance, and tests\n70\t- compact responses prefer the top candidate span handle when one is present\n71\t- the README and MCP docs should mention `plan_edit` as a patch-oriented planning tool\n72\t\n73\tNo automated docs test was applicable for this note.\n74\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"43571d9b-5466-423d-94d5-d70c376b2a86","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.562Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:120:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011nh2BJCzpeGeBHs3Fy9gWy","type":"tool_result","content":"260\t        status_snapshot_from_graph(engine.graph(), include_languages)\n261\t    }\n262\t\n263\t    fn handle_tools_list(&self) -> Value {\n264\t        json!({\n265\t            \"tools\": [\n266\t                {\n267\t                    \"name\": \"get_context_capsule\",\n268\t                    \"description\": \"First discovery tool when you do not yet know which files matter. Returns the most relevant source pivots plus nearby symbols, along with a reusable context_handle and suggested_expand target, so you can avoid broad file reads.\",\n269\t                    \"inputSchema\": {\n270\t                        \"type\": \"object\",\n271\t                        \"properties\": {\n272\t                            \"query\": {\n273\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n274\t                                \"description\": \"Natural language query describing what you need context for\"\n275\t                            },\n276\t                            \"mode\": {\n277\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n278\t                                \"description\": \"Result mode: 'full' (default, multiple pivots + context) or 'focused' (max 1 pivot, max 5 context, minimal budget)\",\n279\t                                \"enum\": [\"full\", \"focused\"],\n280\t                                \"default\": \"full\"\n281\t                            },\n282\t                            \"render\": {\n283\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n284\t                                \"description\": \"Result rendering: 'hybrid' (default markdown summary + JSON payload), 'markdown', or 'json'\",\n285\t                                \"enum\": [\"json\", \"markdown\", \"hybrid\"],\n286\t                                \"default\": \"hybrid\"\n287\t                            }\n288\t                        },\n289\t                        \"required\": [\"query\"]\n290\t                    }\n291\t                },\n292\t                {\n293\t                    \"name\": \"prepare_change\",\n294\t                    \"description\": \"First workflow tool for fix/add/refactor tasks once you know the area. Returns likely edit files, symbols, tests, risks, and reusable memory in one bundle.\",\n295\t                    \"inputSchema\": {\n296\t                        \"type\": \"object\",\n297\t                        \"properties\": {\n298\t                            \"query\": {\n299\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n300\t                                \"description\": \"Natural language task such as 'fix login timeout' or 'add OAuth refresh'\"\n301\t                            },\n302\t                            \"entry_files\": {\n303\t                                \"type\": \"array\",\n304\t                                \"description\": \"Optional files to bias the change plan toward\",\n305\t                                \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n306\t                            },\n307\t                            \"entry_symbols\": {\n308\t                                \"type\": \"array\",\n309\t                                \"description\": \"Optional symbols to bias the change plan toward\",\n310\t                                \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n311\t                            },\n312\t                            \"mode\": {\n313\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n314\t                                \"description\": \"Result mode: 'auto' (default), 'compact', or 'full'\",\n315\t                                \"enum\": [\"auto\", \"compact\", \"full\"],\n316\t                                \"default\": \"auto\"\n317\t                            },\n318\t                            \"budget\": {\n319\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n320\t                                \"description\": \"Output budget: 'tiny', 'compact', or 'full' (default auto chooses for you)\",\n321\t                                \"enum\": [\"tiny\", \"compact\", \"full\"]\n322\t                            },\n323\t                            \"max_tokens\": {\n324\t                                \"type\": \"integer\",\n325\t                                \"description\": \"Optional approximate hard cap for the returned payload\"\n326\t                            },\n327\t                            \"wire_format\": {\n328\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n329\t                                \"description\": \"Response wire format: 'standard' or 'dense' (optional; auto may choose dense for strict budgets)\",\n330\t                                \"enum\": [\"standard\", \"dense\"]\n331\t                            },\n332\t                            \"render\": {\n333\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n334\t                                \"description\": \"Workflow result rendering: 'hybrid' (default markdown summary + JSON payload), 'markdown', or 'json'\",\n335\t                                \"enum\": [\"json\", \"markdown\", \"hybrid\"],\n336\t                                \"default\": \"hybrid\"\n337\t                            }\n338\t                        },\n339\t                        \"required\": [\"query\"]\n340\t                    }\n341\t                },\n342\t                {\n343\t                    \"name\": \"plan_edit\",\n344\t                    \"description\": \"Patch-oriented planning bundle that returns likely edit files, candidate spans, affected callers/dependencies, relevant docs, and recommended tests in one assistant-facing plan.\",\n345\t                    \"inputSchema\": {\n346\t                        \"type\": \"object\",\n347\t                        \"properties\": {\n348\t                            \"query\": {\n349\t                                \"type\": \"string\",\n350\t                                \"description\": \"Natural language task such as 'fix login timeout' or 'add OAuth refresh'\"\n351\t                            },\n352\t                            \"entry_files\": {\n353\t                                \"type\": \"array\",\n354\t                                \"description\": \"Optional files to bias the edit plan toward\",\n355\t                                \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" }\n356\t                            },\n357\t                            \"entry_symbols\": {\n358\t                                \"type\": \"array\",\n359\t                                \"description\": \"Optional symbols to bias the edit plan toward\","}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbdQyD8DbWiNRxRFHe822D","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1ef2446c-65e9-448b-b0b2-a347387b5bdb","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:25.637Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore extension + MCP surface"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:165:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UBfsy7GuEgYNjRQRwFy6vF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Lattice Assistant Usefulness Roadmap\n\n**Date:** 2026-04-11\n**Status:** Executed\n**Audience:** Lattice maintainers building for coding assistants\n\nExecuted across Phases 1-5. This roadmap now serves as the concise source of truth for delivered status, with detailed rationale and implementation notes living in the linked architecture/README updates. Verification was focused and phase-targeted rather than a single full-workspace run.\n\n## Goal\n\nMake Lattice more useful for a coding assistant by shifting it from strong retrieval toward a more deterministic end-to-end coding workflow engine.\n\nPrimary success metrics:\n\n- Reduce ambiguous follow-up expansions\n- Improve code-first retrieval for natural-language and identifier-heavy coding queries\n- Reduce tool calls required to reach an actionable edit plan\n- Improve scenario-level debugging support\n- Make durable memory more trustworthy and explainable\n\n## Baseline\n\nCurrent strengths:\n\n- `get_context_capsule`, `prepare_change`, and related workflow tools already compress discovery and planning well\n- `expand_context` already supports handle-based follow-up expansion for cached workflow results\n- semantic search now has persisted ANN support with SQLite fallback\n- memory is persistent, refreshable, stale-aware, and searchable\n\nCurrent gaps:","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"a76d85a3-da66-4a28-8af3-ad6c9145ab2c","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:30.691Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:210:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0128HtZ1gAsC7uitiY3J6SPg","type":"tool_result","content":"# Patch-Oriented Planning\n\n## Summary\n\nPhase 3 adds `plan_edit`, a patch-oriented planning workflow that sits on top of the existing change-planning stack and returns a more edit-ready bundle for assistants. The goal is to move from \"here are the likely files\" to \"here is the first patch plan,\" while staying compact enough to use directly in an edit loop.\n\n## Why This Exists\n\n`prepare_change` is useful for finding the likely area to edit, but assistants still need additional structure before they can safely patch code:\n\n- which symbols are the best first edit anchors\n- which line spans should be touched first\n- which callers or dependency contracts are likely to move\n- which docs and tests are likely to go stale\n- which compact response is still small enough to apply without another discovery round\n\n`plan_edit` packages that information in one bundle so the assistant can move from task statement to concrete patch plan with fewer follow-up calls.\n\n## Bundle Shape\n\n`PlanEditBundle` currently includes:\n\n- `edit_files`\n- `supporting_files`\n- `symbols`\n- `candidate_spans`\n- `affected_callers`\n- `affected_dependencies`\n- `relevant_docs`\n- `stale_doc_signals`\n- `tests`\n- `test_gaps`\n- `matched_rules`\n- `memories`\n- `memory_highlights`\n- `risks`\n- `rationale`\n- `stats` in full mode\n\nCandidate spans carry file, symbol, line range, confidence, and stable symbol handles when available. The daemon prefers the top candidate span handle for `suggested_expand`; when one is not available, it falls back to the underlying change-plan suggestion.\n\n## Compactness And Fallbacks\n\nThe daemon exposes both compact and full `plan_edit` delivery modes. Compact mode trims supporting material while preserving patch anchors, docs signals, and tests. Full mode keeps the wider stats bundle for inspection.\n\nThe same backward-compatible handle discipline used elsewhere in Lattice applies here as well:\n\n- stable symbol handles are preferred when the graph node is known\n- legacy `symbol:` and `file:` forms remain accepted in the surrounding workflow plumbing\n- cached context handles continue to work across `expand_context`\n\n## Current Implementation Touchpoints\n\nThe implementation now lives in:\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/mod.rs`\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`\n\nThe daemon layer adds the MCP tool declaration, request handling, compact/full shaping, context-handle seeding, and tests for `tools/call` behavior.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbdQyD8DbWiNRxRFHe822D","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"95c4a1eb-bdeb-4a63-a049-79d59286e171","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:39.932Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore extension + MCP surface"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:248:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile the report:\n\n## Lattice Documentation Audit Report\n\n### 1. Architecture Files (docs/architecture/)\n\nAll five files are recent (April 2026) design/implementation notes:\n\n- **2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md** — Workflow for `plan_edit`: patch-oriented edit planning that sits on top of change-planning, returning first-edit anchors, caller impacts, stale-doc signals, and candidate spans before full code review.\n- **2026-04-11-structured-memory.md** — Phase 5 memory model: adds structured assertion metadata (verification status, freshness policy, provenance, evidence, contradiction/supersession links) on top of durable store; enables trustworthy memory reuse with explainable ranking.\n- **2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md** — Phase 1 identity system: stable handles (`SymbolId` derived from file, name, byte_offset) for follow-up expansion, replacing ambiguous name-only targets; backward-compatible with legacy forms.\n- **2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md** — `trace_scenario` workflow: behavior-focused debugging that returns execution paths, guards, side effects, and failure branches ranked by likelihood without exhaustive coverage claims.\n- **2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md** — Vector indexing redesign: USearch ANN backend (`.lattice/vectors.usearch`) alongside SQLite, FTS5 for memory keyword search, scope-aware retrieval (symbol vs. file-summary vectors), incremental sync on graph changes.\n\n### 2. Plans (docs/plans/) — Current & Archive\n\n**Current fork plan (largest, 968 lines):**\n- **2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md** — Successor system design for major fork: unifies workspace graph (files, symbols, docs, tests, configs), append-only event log, typed memory graph (observation, decision, constraint, pattern, anti-pattern, workflow outcome, failure pattern, procedure, preference, architecture invariant, docs contract, counter-memory), consolidation engine, retrieval engine V1, verification engine. 11-phase roadmap with identity model, event log substrate, memory graph storage, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, workflow engine V2, metrics/evaluation, human review UI, and hardening. Includes measurable success criteria (30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, zero stale/contradicted memory in trusted surfaces).\n\n**Archive/foundational plans:**\n- **2026-04-11-assistant-usefulness-roadmap.md** (174 lines) — Executed Phases 1-5 roadmap focusing on stable handles, scenario tracing, patch-oriented planning, structured memory, and storage backends.\n- **2026-03-13-agent-workflow-roadmap.md** (215 lines) — In-progress roadmap shifting from retrieval to workflow engine; targets reduction in tool round-trips, repeated context, and durable memory improvements.\n- **2026-03-30-unified-knowledge-graph-design.md** (532 lines) — Proposed expansion of code graph to unify docs, Markdown, ADRs, runbooks, memory, and freshness across one local knowledge graph.\n- **2026-02-25-lattice-design.md** (484 lines) — Original approved design doc: problem statement (token waste on irrelevant code), solution (local tree-sitter graph, MCP server, SQLite + vectors, no external APIs).\n- **2026-02-25-lattice-implementation.md** (6,447 lines) — Detailed implementation plan with symbol indexing, graph traversal, embedding pipeline, context caching, query engine, MCP contract, extension UI, tests, and deployment.\n\n### 3. Operator Guides / Runbooks / Migration Guides\n\nNone exist yet. The codebase includes:\n- **Build/deploy snippet in CLAUDE.md** (lines 54-68) — Daemon build, extension npm, test, and deploy steps.\n- **Benchmark test files** — `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` (test code, not standalone guides).\n\n### 4. Benchmark / Evaluation Docs\n\nNo standalone evaluation guides. The fork plan (Section 2.2.9) defines benchmark requirements:\n- retrieval relevance, token efficiency, stale-memory surfacing, test-pass-after-plan, repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- 30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision targets\n- Golden benchmark tasks not yet documented separately.\n\n### 5. CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md Highlights\n\n**CLAUDE.md (69 lines):**\n- Durable instruction surface for Lattice repo behavior\n- Core rules: production-grade first pass, correctness over convenience, workspace safety, error handling as core design, verification before completion\n- Communication style: direct, factual, critical by default; no empty praise\n- Markdown heading references: cite exact doc paths when architecture/behavior sourced from docs\n- Build/deploy steps, project structure (daemon/extension split), testing discipline\n\n**AGENTS.md (10,934 bytes, detailed):**\n- Codex-specific instruction surface for repo-wide behavior\n- Lattice workflow tools priority: `prepare_change` for implementation, `get_context_capsule` for understanding, `diagnose_failure` for failures/traces\n- Backward-compatibility and contract discipline: no silent behavior changes; if MCP shape/workflow metadata must change, update docs and migrations together\n- Documentation rules: update README on public behavior change, update CLAUDE.md on Codex rules change, add durable design notes under `docs/` for architecture/memory/ranking/workflow changes\n- Error handling, definition of done, workspace safety, observability, performance/scale, and project structure sections\n- Explicit prohibition on self-attribution to commits, verification before claiming coverage, and test discipline requirements\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Documented:** Architecture (5 files on Phases 1-5 and Phase 4 storage redesign), design lineage (5 plan files from Feb-May 2026), project instructions (CLAUDE.md for Lattice, AGENTS.md for Codex).\n\n**Not Yet Documented:** Operator guides, migration pathways, standalone evaluation/benchmark suite, runbooks, upgrade procedures.\n\n**For the fork:** The cognitive workspace plan is comprehensive but requires creating migration docs (from current Lattice), operator guides (memory review surface, event log inspection, consolidation monitoring), and a benchmark suite with golden tasks. None of those docs exist yet."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2962a26e-f7ea-4d38-93af-b7e898b6a06a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:57.338Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the Lattice docs at /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/. Report:\n\n1. All files under docs/architecture/ with a one-line description of each.\n2. All files under docs/plans/ (current and archive) — relevant prior plans for memory, retrieval, event log, consolidation, verification, identity, fork.\n3. Any operator guides, runbooks, or migration guides.\n4. Any benchmark or evaluation docs.\n5. Existing CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md content highlights (what conventions and rules they prescribe).\n\nThis will inform a new build plan for a major fork. The goal is to understand what's already documented and what new docs need creating. Report in under 600 words.","agentId":"adb617741e2078de2","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile the report:\n\n## Lattice Documentation Audit Report\n\n### 1. Architecture Files (docs/architecture/)\n\nAll five files are recent (April 2026) design/implementation notes:\n\n- **2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md** — Workflow for `plan_edit`: patch-oriented edit planning that sits on top of change-planning, returning first-edit anchors, caller impacts, stale-doc signals, and candidate spans before full